* VTY Key Index::               An item for each VTY key.


File: zebra.info,  Node: Overview,  Next: Installation,  Prev: Top,  Up: Top

Overview
********

   Zebra is free routing software that manages TCP/IP based routing
protocols.  IPv6 routing protocols are also supported.  Zebra is
distributed under GNU General Public License (*note Copying::.).

   You can get the latest information from Zebra web page:
`http://www.zebra.org/'.

* Menu:

* What is Zebra::               What is Zebra?
* System architecture::         Zebra system architecture
* Mailing list::                Mailing list information.
* Bug report::                  How to report bug.


File: zebra.info,  Node: What is Zebra,  Next: System architecture,  Prev: Overview,  Up: Overview

What is Zebra
=============

   Today, TCP/IP networks are covering all of the world.  The Internet
has been in deployed many countries, companies and at home.  If you
connect to the Internet you use TCP/IP routing functions.  Zebra is a
free TCP/IP routing software package.  A Zebra installed box works as a
dedicated router.  With Zebra your machine talks with other routers by
means of routing protocols and updates the kernel routing table. You can
dynamically change the configuration and view routing table information
from the Zebra terminal interface.

   For a small network or a stub network there is no need for routing
software.  The only thing you have to do is to set up the interfaces and
put a few command about static route and/or default route.  But if the
network is rather large or if the network structure changes frequently,
you will need dynamic routing protocols such as RIP, OSPF or BGP.  Zebra
is for you

   Below is the list of currently supported routing protocol RFCs.

`RFC1771'
     A Border Gateway Protocol 4 (BGP-4). Y. Rekhter & T. Li. March
     1995.

`RFC1996'
     BGP Route Reflection An alternative to full mesh IBGP. T. Bates &
     R. Chandrasekeran. June 1996.

`RFC1997'
     BGP Communities Attribute. R. Chandra, P. Traina & T. Li. August
     1996.

`RFC2080'
     RIPng for IPv6. G. Malkin, R. Minnear. January 1997.

`RFC2283'
     Multiprotocol Extensions for BGP-4. T. Bates, R. Chandra, D.  Katz,
     Y. Rekhter. February 1998.

`RFC2328'
     OSPF Version 2. J. Moy. April 1998.

`RFC2453'
     RIP Version 2. G. Malkin. November 1998.

`RFC2545'
     Use of BGP-4 Multiprotocol Extensions for IPv6 Inter-Domain
     Routing. P. Marques, F. Dupont. March 1999.


File: zebra.info,  Node: System architecture,  Next: Mailing list,  Prev: What is Zebra,  Up: Overview

System Architecture
===================

   Traditional routing software are made as a one process program which
provides all of the routing protocol functionalities as a whole.  Zebra
takes different approach, it is made from collection of some daemons.
There are some protocol specific daemons and a kernel routing table
manager.

   `ripd' handles RIP protocol. `ospfd' is a daemon which supports OSPF
version 2.  `bgpd' supports BGP-4 protocol.  For changing the kernel
routing table and for redistribution between different routing
protocols, there is `zebra' daemon.  There is no need for these daemons
to be running over the same machine.  This architecture makes new
possibility of the routing system.  It is pretty easy adding new
routing protocol daemon to the system.

     +----+  +----+  +-----+  +-----+
     |bgpd|  |ripd|  |ospfd|  |zebra|
     +----+  +----+  +-----+  +-----+
                                 |
     +---------------------------|--+
     |                           v  |
     |  UNIX Kernel  routing table  |
     |                              |
     +------------------------------+
     
         Zebra System Architecture

   Zebra use a multi-thread mechanism when it runs over a multi-thread
supported kernel.  But at this moment thread library which comes with
GNU/Linux or FreeBSD has some problem for running Zebra, so we don't use
thread on those OSes, instead we use select system call for
multiplexing.

   When zebra runs under GNU Hurd kernel it will act as a kernel routing
table itself.  Under GNU Hurd all TCP/IP services are provided by user
processes called `pfinet'.  Zebra will provide all the routing
selection mechanisms for the process.  This feature will be implemented
in the future.


File: zebra.info,  Node: Mailing list,  Next: Bug report,  Prev: System architecture,  Up: Overview

Mailing list
============

   There is a mailing list for discussing about Zebra.  If you have any
comments or suggestions to Zebra please send to <zebra@zebra.org>.  New
snapshot announcement, improvement notes, some patches are sent to the
list.

   To subscribe Zebra mailing list <zebra@zebra.org>, please send a
mail to
<majordomo@zebra.org> with body includes only

     subscribe zebra

   To unsubscribe the list, please send a mail to <majordomo@zebra.org>
with body includes only

     unsubscribe zebra


File: zebra.info,  Node: Bug report,  Prev: Mailing list,  Up: Overview

Bug report
==========

   If you think you find a bug, please send a bug report to
<bug-zebra@gnu.org>.  Zebra maintainers subscribe the list.  When you
send a bug report, please be careful about below points.

   * Please note what kind of OS are you using.  If you use IPv6 stack
     please note about that.

   * Please show us the result of `netstat -rn' and `ifconfig -a'.
     Information from zebra's VTY command `show ip route' will also
     helpful.

   * Please send your configuration file with the report.  If you
     specify the argument to the configure script please note about
     that.

   Bug report is very important for us for improving the quality of
Zebra.  Zebra is still in development stage but please don't hesitate to
send a bug report to <bug-zebra@gnu.org>.


File: zebra.info,  Node: Installation,  Next: Configuration File,  Prev: Overview,  Up: Top

Installation
************

   Zebra is in development stage, so there is no official release in the
past.  Zebra's beta package can be found at
`ftp://ftp.zebra.org/pub/zebra/'.  Once it is released it will be found
at GNU FTP archive sites.

   Zebra installation is very easy by configure script.  It
automatically detects current status of the machine so please check
kernel configuration before run configure script.

* Menu:

* GNU/Linux configuration::
* Configure options::
* VTY port setup::


File: zebra.info,  Node: GNU/Linux configuration,  Next: Configure options,  Prev: Installation,  Up: Installation

GNU/Linux configuration
=======================

   GNU/Linux has very flexible kernel configuration feature.  If you use
GNU/Linux, make it sure current kernel configuration is what you want.
Zebra will run with any kernel configuration but some recommendation
exists.

CONFIG_NETLINK
     Kernel/User netlink socket.  This is a brand new feature which
     enable advanced Kernel between Zebra interface (*note Linux
     netlink::.).

CONFIG_RTNETLINK
     Routing messages.  This makes it possible netlink routing message.
     If you specify this option, `zebra' can detect routing information
     updates from the kernel (*note Kernel interface::.).

CONFIG_IP_MULTICAST
     IP: multicasting.  This option should be specified when you use
     `ripd' or `ospfd' because these protocol use multicast.

   IPv6 supported is added from GNU/Linux kernel version 2.2.  If you
try to use Zebra IPv6 feature on GNU/Linux kernel, please make it sure
below library has been installed.

`inet6-apps'
     `inet6-apps' package includes basic IPv6 related library such as
     `inet_ntop' and `inet_pton'.  Some basic IPv6 programs such as
     `ping', `ftp', `inetd' also included. `inet-apps' can be found at
     `ftp://ftp.inner.net/pub/ipv6/'.

`net-tools'
     `net-tools' provide IPv6 enabled interface and routing utility.  It
     contains `ifconfig', `route', `netstat' and other tools.
     `net-tools' can be found at `ftp://somewhere/'.


File: zebra.info,  Node: Configure options,  Next: VTY port setup,  Prev: GNU/Linux configuration,  Up: Installation

Configure options
=================

   Normally Zebra configure detects host configuration automatically.
For manual configuration there are several configure options.  You can
turn off IPv6 support or disable of making some daemons by configure
options.

`--enable-guile'
     Turn on making zebra-guile interpreter.  You will need guile
     library to make this.

`--disable-ipv6'
     Turn off IPv6 related features and daemons.

`--disable-zebra'
     Do not build zebra daemon.

`--disable-bgpd'
     Do not build bgpd.

`--disable-ripd'
     Do not build ripd.

`--disable-ripngd'
     Do not build ripngd.

`--disable-ospfd'
     Do not build ospfd.

`--disable-ospf6d'
     Do not build ospf6d.

`--disable-pthread'
     Do not use pthread features.  Default is disable so you don't need
     specify it.

   You can specify any of above option to the configure script.
Configuration and making program is very simple.  You run `configure'
and then `make' Zebra:

     % ./configure --disable-ipv6
     % make

   will create zebra and other beasts.  Installation is done by:

     % make install

   This will install all of Zebra software under PREFIX/sbin.  Sample
configuration installed under PREFIX/etc.  If you want to change the
installation directory.  Please specify below option to the configure.

`--prefix=PREFIX'
     Install architecture-independent files in PREFIX [/usr/local].

`--sysconfdir=DIR'
     Read-only sample configuration file in DIR [PREFIX/etc].


File: zebra.info,  Node: VTY port setup,  Prev: Configure options,  Up: Installation

VTY port setup
==============

   Zebra daemon's has each own terminal interface which called VTY.  So
after Zebra beasts installation you have to setup each program's port
number for connecting to daemons.  Please add below entry to
`/etc/services'.

     zebrasrv      2600/tcp		  # zebra service
     zebra         2601/tcp		  # zebra vty
     ripd          2602/tcp		  # RIPd vty
     ripngd        2603/tcp		  # RIPngd vty
     ospfd         2604/tcp		  # OSPFd vty
     bgpd          2605/tcp		  # BGPd vty
     ospf6d        2606/tcp		  # OSPF6d vty

   If you use FreeBSD newer than 2.2.8, above entry is already added to
`/etc/services'.  So there is no need of adding.


File: zebra.info,  Node: Configuration File,  Next: VTY,  Prev: Installation,  Up: Top

Configuration File
******************

   Zebra daemon has it's own configuration file.  For example zebra's
default configuration file name is `zebra.conf'.  Program name plus
`.conf' is default configuration file name. You can specify
configuration file by -f or -config-file option to the daemon.

   In configuration file, you can write debug option, vty's password,
routing daemon configuration, logfile name and so on.

* Menu:

* Basic configuration::
* Sample Configuration::


File: zebra.info,  Node: Basic configuration,  Next: Sample Configuration,  Prev: Configuration File,  Up: Configuration File

Basic configuration
===================

 - Command: hostname HOSTNAME
     Set hostname of the router.

 - Command: password PASSWORD
     Set password for vty interface.  If there is no password vty don't
     accept connection.

 - Command: enable password PASSWORD
     Set enable password.

 - Command: lines LINES
     Override number of terminal lines for vty interface.  Set it to
     zero to avoid pagination.

 - Command: log stdout
 - Command: no log stdout
     Set loggin output to stdout.

 - Command: log file FILENAME
     If you want to log into the file please specify `filename' as
     follow.
          log file /usr/local/etc/bgpd.log

 - Command: log syslog
 - Command: no log syslog
     Set loggin output to syslog.

 - Command: write terminal
     Display current configuration to the vty interface.

 - Command: write file
     Write current configuration to configuration file.

 - Command: config terminal

 - Command: who

 - Command: list
     List commands.

 - Command: service password-encryption
     Encrypt password.

 - Command: service advanced-vty
     Enable advanced mode VTY.

 - Command: show version
     Show the current version of the Zebra and it's build host
     information.

 - Command: line vty
     Enter vty configuration mode

 - Command: banner motd default
     Set default motd string.

 - Command: no banner motd
     No motd banner string print out.

 - Line Command: exec-timeout SECOND
     Set time out seconds.

 - Line Command: access-class ACCESS-LIST
     Restrict vty connection with access list.


File: zebra.info,  Node: Sample Configuration,  Prev: Basic configuration,  Up: Configuration File

Sample Configuration
====================

   Below is sample configuration of zebra daemon.

     !
     ! Zebra configuration file
     !
     hostname Router
     password zebra
     enable password zebra
     !
     log stdout

   '!' and '#' are comment character.  If the first character of the
word is one of comment character, from the character to the end of line
is ignored as comment.

     password zebra!password

   If comment character is not the first character of the word, it's a
normal character. So above '!' doesn't regard as comment and password is
set to 'zebra!password'.


