zebra terminal mode commands
============================

 - Command: show ip route
     Display current routes which zebra hold.

          Router# show ip route
          Codes: K - kernel route, C - connected, S - static, R - RIP,
                 B - BGP * - FIB route.
          
          K* 0.0.0.0/0              203.181.89.241
          S  0.0.0.0/0              203.181.89.1
          C* 127.0.0.0/8            lo
          C* 203.181.89.240/28      eth0

 - Command: show ipv6 route

 - Command: show interface

 - Command: show ipforward
     Display host's IP forward function is enabled or not.  Almost UNIX
     kernel can be configured as IP forward disable.  If so the box
     can't work as a router.

 - Command: show ipv6forward
     Display host's IP version 6 forward is enabled or not.


File: zebra.info,  Node: ripd,  Next: ripngd,  Prev: zebra,  Up: Top

ripd
****

   The Routing Information Protocol RIP is legendary protocol.  It is a
very simple distance vector routing protocol. `ripd' supports RIP
version 2 described in RFC2453 and RIP version 1 described in RFC1058.
RIP is a "distance-vector" protocol, based on the "Bellman-Ford"
algorithms.  The RIP protocol was developed in the 1970s at Xerox labs
as part of the XNS routing protocol.  As a distance-vector protocol,
routers running RIP will send updates to its neighbors thus allowing the
convergence to a known topology.  In each update, the distance of any
given router will be broadcast to its neighbor.

   The following points are important to consider in the implementation
of ripd:

   One of the characteristics of ripd used in Zebra is that the user
must have a root privilege.  `ripd' uses port 521, but in order to bind
this port, a privilege is needed.

   Netmask of ripd supports both version 1 and version 2 of RIP.
Version 1 of RIP originally contained no netmask information.  In RIP
version 1, classes were originally used to determine the size of the
network mask.  Class A networks use 8 bits of mask, Class B networks
use 16 bits of masks, while Class C networks use 24 bits of mask.
Today, the most widely used method of a network mask is assigned to the
packet in the basis of the interface that received the packet.  Version
2 of RIP supports a variable length subnet mask (VLSM).  By extending
the subnet mask, the mask can be divided and reused.  Each subnet can
be used for different purposes such as large to middle size LANs and
WAN links.  Zebra ripd does not support the non-sequential net masks
that are included in RIP Version 2.

   In a case of the same information with the same prefix and metric,
the old information will be suppressed.  Ripd does not currently support
equal cost multipath routing.

* Menu:

* Invoking ripd::
* ripd configuration::
* Filtering RIP route::
* ripd terminal mode commands::
* ripd debug commands::


