bgpd
****

   bgpd is a Border Gateway Protocol 4 (BGP-4) protocol daemon.  BGP-4
is described in RFC1771.  bgpd also supports Multi Protocol Extension
for BGP-4 (sometimes called as BGP-4+ or MBGP) which described in
RFC2283.

   BGP-4 is one of the EGP (Exterior Gateway Protocol) and used for
inter domain routing.

* Menu:

* Invoking bgpd::
* bgpd configuration::
* bgpd terminal mode commands::
* bgpd sample session::
* bgpd log format::
* How to set up 6bone connection::


File: zebra.info,  Node: Invoking bgpd,  Next: bgpd configuration,  Prev: bgpd,  Up: bgpd

Invoking bgpd
=============

   `bgpd' specific invoking options.  Common options also can be
specified (*note Common invoking options::.).

`-p PORT'
`--bgp_port=PORT'
     Set bgp protocol's port number.

`-r'
`--retain'
     When program terminates, retain added route by zebra.


File: zebra.info,  Node: bgpd configuration,  Next: bgpd terminal mode commands,  Prev: Invoking bgpd,  Up: bgpd

bgpd configuration
==================

   There is some commands to configure bgpd.  Default configuration file
is `bgpd.conf'.

 - Command: router zebra
     With this statement, BGP routes go to the `zebra' daemon.  If you
     want to install BGP routes into the kernel.  You have to specify
     this command.

 - Command: router bgp AS-NUMBER
     Enable BGP protocol process with specified AS-NUMBER.  After this
     statement you can input `BGP Command' listed below.

   In `router bgp' clause there are neighbor specific configuration.

 - BGP Command: bgp router-id ROUTER-ID
     This command specify router ID of the router.  If bgpd connect to
     zebra it gets interface and address information.  In that case
     default router ID value is selected as the largest IP Address of
     the interfaces.  When `router zebra' is not enabled bgpd can't get
     interface information so `router-id' is set to 0.0.0.0.  So please
     set router-id by yourself.

 - BGP Command: network NETWORK
     This command add the announcement network.
          router bgp 1
           network 10.0.0.0/8
     This configuration means network 10.0.0.0/8 will be announced to
     the all neighbors.  Some vendor router don't advertize routes
     which doesn't exist in IGP routing table but bgp doesn't care
     about IGP routing table when announce the route.

 - BGP Command: aggregate-address NETWORK
     This command specify aggregate address.

 - BGP Command: neighbor PEER remote-as AS-NUMBER
     Create new neighbor as remote-as is AS-NUMBER.  PEER can be IPv4
     address or IPv6 address.
          router bgp 1
           neighbor 10.0.0.1 remote-as 2
     In this case my router try to peer with AS2.

     This command must be the first command to configure neighbor.  If
     remote-as is not specified, bgpd will complain like this
          can't find neighbor 10.0.0.1

 - BGP Command: neighbor PEER next-hop-self
     This command specify announce route's nexthop to self address.

 - BGP Command: neighbor PEER shutdown
 - BGP Command: no neighbor PEER shutdown
     Shutdown the peer.  We can delete the neighbor's configuration by
     `no neighbor PEER remote-as AS-NUMBER' but all the configuration
     of the neighbor will be deleted.  When one want to preserve the
     configuration but want to drop BGP peer, please use this command.

 - BGP Command: neighbor PEER description ...
 - BGP Command: no neighbor PEER description ...
     Set description to the peer.

 - BGP Command: neighbor PEER distribute-list DIRECT
     This command specify distribute-list to the peer.  DIRECT is `in'
     or `out'.

 - BGP Command: neighbor PEER route-map DIRECT
     Apply route-map to the neighbor.  DIRECT must be `in' or `out'.

 - BGP Command: neighbor PEER version VERSION
     Set up neighbor's BGP version.  VERSION can be 4, 4+ or 4-.  BGP
     version 4 is default value of the BGP peering.  BGP version 4+
     means that the neighbor supports Multiprotocol Extensions for
     BGP-4.  BGP version 4- is similar but the neighbor speaks old
     Internet-Draft revision 00's Multiprotocol Extensions for BGP-4.
     Some routing software is still using this version.

 - BGP Command: neighbor PEER ebgp-multihop

 - BGP Command: neighbor PEER interface IFNAME


File: zebra.info,  Node: bgpd terminal mode commands,  Next: bgpd sample session,  Prev: bgpd configuration,  Up: bgpd

